WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.067
Positional encoding.

2
00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:04.653
Attention cannot tell first from last.

3
00:00:04.653 --> 00:00:07.300
Here is how transformers learn order anyway.

4
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.367
Attention scores pairs of vectors, but it has no idea of order.

5
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:15.333
So to a raw transformer, dog bites man...

6
00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:17.300
is literally identical to...

7
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.533
man bites dog.

8
00:00:18.533 --> 00:00:22.000
The fix is to give every position its own signature.

9
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.633
The 2017 paper added sine and cosine waves to each embedding.

10
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:29.633
A fast wave changes quickly.

11
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:31.767
A slower wave changes gently.

12
00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:34.042
A very slow wave barely moves.

13
00:00:34.042 --> 00:00:37.333
Together, each position gets a unique fingerprint.

14
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:40.267
There are three generations to know.

15
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.800
Sinusoidal uses those fixed waves.

16
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.467
Learned trains an embedding per position, but positions past the training length do not exist.

17
00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:55.100
RoPE, used by Llama, Mistral and Qwen, rotates queries and keys by a position-based angle.

18
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.800
Let's see the rotary idea.

19
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.600
A token at position one points this way.

20
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.600
At position two, we rotate it a little further.

21
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.933
At position three, further again.

22
00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:12.967
The angle between two tokens reflects their distance, so attention sees relative position by construction.

23
00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:16.800
In code, it is just a few lines of NumPy.

24
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.033
We build a grid with positions down the side and dimensions across.

25
00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:25.200
The angle shrinks as the dimension grows, so wavelengths get longer.

26
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.230
Even columns get sine, odd get cosine.

27
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.667
Every row is unique.

28
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:32.700
Why does this matter in practice?

29
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.800
Two reports can share the same words: thruster fired after the leak was sealed, or the reverse.

30
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.198
They tell opposite failure stories.

31
00:01:41.198 --> 00:01:45.033
When a model gets causality right, positions are doing the work.

32
00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:48.299
So check rope theta before feeding long reports.

33
00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:52.500
Models stretched past trained positions quietly lose this reasoning.

34
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.509
Let's recap.

35
00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:56.800
Attention alone is blind to order.

36
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.367
Positional encoding gives each position a signature.

37
00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:04.300
Remember the three generations: sinusoidal, learned, and RoPE.

38
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.383
And long context only works if positions stay in range.

39
00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:10.033
Next up: self-attention.
